1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,717
Is everything legal also right?

2
00:00:03,169 --> 00:00:05,752
Most of the time, we hope the two go together.

3
00:00:06,195 --> 00:00:08,887
But history shows they can be far apart.

4
00:00:10,117 --> 00:00:15,947
Aristotle distinguished between justice in how a society shares out goods and burdens,

5
00:00:15,837 --> 00:00:18,707
and justice in how it repairs a wrong.

6
00:00:19,198 --> 00:00:21,291
Modern thinkers add another question:

7
00:00:21,694 --> 00:00:25,271
are decisions made by fair and impartial procedures?

8
00:00:25,938 --> 00:00:30,052
Law is one of the tools a society uses to pursue justice.

9
00:00:30,486 --> 00:00:33,359
It is not the same thing as justice itself.

10
00:00:34,050 --> 00:00:38,197
Philosophers disagree about how law and morality are connected.

11
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:43,321
Legal positivists hold that whether a rule is valid law is one question,

12
00:00:43,585 --> 00:00:45,592
and whether it is good is another.

13
00:00:46,221 --> 00:00:49,220
Natural-law thinkers, such as Thomas Aquinas,

14
00:00:49,511 --> 00:00:54,859
argued that a seriously unjust human law loses its claim on our conscience.

15
00:00:55,229 --> 00:00:59,154
Both accept that a rule can be enforced and still be wrong.

16
00:00:59,783 --> 00:01:05,284
In the American South, racial segregation was written into law for decades.

17
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:11,296
In 1896, the Supreme Court accepted the doctrine of separate but equal.

18
00:01:11,703 --> 00:01:15,522
In 1954, in Brown v. Board of Education,

19
00:01:15,731 --> 00:01:19,374
it ruled that separate schools are inherently unequal.

20
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:25,887
In April 1963, Martin Luther King Jr. was jailed in Birmingham, Alabama,

21
00:01:26,107 --> 00:01:28,589
after marching in defiance of a court order.

22
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:31,440
In his Letter from Birmingham Jail,

23
00:01:31,694 --> 00:01:36,637
he distinguished just laws from unjust ones and, citing Augustine,

24
00:01:36,836 --> 00:01:40,030
wrote that an unjust law is no law at all.

25
00:01:40,544 --> 00:01:46,367
He also argued that anyone who breaks an unjust law must do so openly and peacefully,

26
00:01:46,631 --> 00:01:48,596
and be willing to accept the penalty.

27
00:01:49,319 --> 00:01:54,746
In 1964, the Civil Rights Act outlawed segregation in public places.

28
00:01:55,117 --> 00:01:59,323
Laws protect people only when institutions apply them fairly.

29
00:01:59,822 --> 00:02:03,763
The rule of law means that everyone, including those in power,

30
00:02:03,927 --> 00:02:08,628
is accountable to the law; that courts are independent and impartial;

31
00:02:08,847 --> 00:02:12,531
and that people are presumed innocent until proven guilty.

32
00:02:13,198 --> 00:02:15,697
The Universal Declaration of Human Rights,

33
00:02:15,587 --> 00:02:20,594
adopted by the UN General Assembly in 1948, states these principles.

34
00:02:20,949 --> 00:02:23,761
It is a declaration, not a treaty.

35
00:02:24,383 --> 00:02:28,516
For the Journal, justice is not merely a human invention.

36
00:02:28,945 --> 00:02:33,324
The prophet Micah summed up what God requires: to do justice,

37
00:02:33,399 --> 00:02:37,119
and to love kindness, and to walk humbly with your God.

38
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:40,880
Scripture warns those who make unjust decrees,

39
00:02:40,974 --> 00:02:44,882
and calls judges to be impartial and to refuse bribes.

40
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:47,448
A society needs laws.

41
00:02:47,875 --> 00:02:50,983
But laws are not the final measure of what is right.

42
00:02:51,504 --> 00:02:55,567
So the question remains: if something is permitted by law,

43
00:02:55,791 --> 00:02:56,961
does that make it right?
